# Notice: third-party sources this repo builds on

This file lists every outside source the work depends on, with what we use
and what we do not take.

## ACI 440.2R-17

Guide for the Design and Construction of Externally Bonded FRP Systems for
Strengthening Concrete Structures. American Concrete Institute, Committee 440,
2017.

- What we use: the design method, its equations, and its section numbers,
  including the stress block factors of Chapter 10 for the strengthened
  section.
- What we do not take: the text, tables, and figures of the guide. The guide
  is copyrighted by ACI and is not freely reusable. Buy or license it from
  ACI for the full method.
- Numbers from the guide, such as a material factor, are facts of the method.
  Each is to be cited to its section. That is not done yet: the checker's
  constants and checks carry no section, equation, or table number, and only
  the check-level sources in the Track 1 PROOF.md are cited. Open, see
  #12.

## ACI 318-14

Building Code Requirements for Structural Concrete. American Concrete
Institute, Committee 318.

- What we use: the concrete stress block factors and steel rules that
  ACI 440.2R-17 points to (the Whitney stress block for the existing,
  unstrengthened section, beta1, minimum steel, and the strength reduction
  factor), and the load combinations of Table 5.3.1 for the design demand.
  The checker uses only the 1.2D + 1.6L form from that table and does not
  check 1.4D yet (issue #4 item 7).
- Edition: the checker's ACI 318 rules match ACI 318-14. Later editions
  supersede it. The ACI 318-19 form of the strength reduction factor
  differs, which would change the existing-section phi only; the
  strengthened-section phi follows ACI 440.2R-17.
- Copyrighted by ACI. Cited, not copied.

## ACI 562-16

Code Requirements for Assessment, Repair, and Rehabilitation of Existing
Concrete Structures. American Concrete Institute, Committee 562, 2016.

- What we use: the strengthening-limit form of Section 5.5.2, which the
  Track 1 worked example cites for its strengthening limit check. ACI's
  provisional text gives it as Eq. (5.5.2), phi Rn >= 1.1D + 0.75L + 0.2S
  (concrete.org/Portals/0/Files/PDF/562-16_Provisional.pdf). ACI's webinar
  slides give it as Eqs. (5.5.2a) 1.1D + 0.5L + 0.2S and (5.5.2b)
  1.1D + 0.75L
  (concrete.org/portals/0/files/pdf/webinars/ws_2017_0417_Alkhrdaji_DSREARS.pdf).
  The published code has not been read here. With no snow load, either
  form contains the same combination as ACI 440.2R-17 Eq. (9.2). Section 5.5.2.1, which
  raises the live load factor to 1.0 for sustained live load, is not modelled
  yet (see #8). The 2013 edition, ACI 562-13, is named only to
  place the 1.2D + 0.5L + Ak + 0.2S combination, its Eq. (5.5.1).
- What we do not take: the text of the code. It is copyrighted by ACI.
  Cited, not copied. ACI has published a provisional text of the 2016
  edition at concrete.org.

## Simpson Strong-Tie worked example

Flexural Strengthening Design Example, Composite Strengthening Systems (CSS),
Simpson Strong-Tie, 2020. A five-page worked example that follows
ACI 440.2R-17 step by step.

- What we use: its input values and its printed results, as the test set for
  Track 1. Printed numbers are recorded in tracks/track-1-flexure-example.md.
  They are not yet recorded with the page they came from. Page citations are
  an open obligation in that file (issue #5 item 5).
- What we do not take: its text, layout, and figures. Copyright stays with
  Simpson Strong-Tie.
- Flag: do not assume this document is freely reusable. Cite it, link to the
  publisher, and record only the numbers a checker needs. No publisher link
  or document number is recorded yet; that is open too (issue #5 item 7).

## Editions

ACI PRC-440.2-23 supersedes ACI 440.2R-17, and ACI CODE-562-21 supersedes
ACI 562-16. This repo deliberately follows the 2017 and 2016 editions,
because the Track 1 worked example uses them. The differences between those
editions and the later ones have not been reviewed yet.

## Rule for new sources

Add every new dataset, file, or example here before code that uses it lands.
Give the source, the licence if one is stated, and what must be preserved.
